{
  "image_name": "2aa7cc5065525a93bf885714196d1a6b.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure compares signaling in dioestrus versus pro-oestrus and shows how hormonal and synaptic inputs appear to regulate nitric oxide production and cytoskeletal remodeling in a reproductive neuroendocrine cell. The membrane contains an NMDA receptor, the kisspeptin receptor GPR54, leptin receptor, and estrogen-sensitive pathways, all converging on nNOS.\n\nIn both states, glutamate activates NMDAR and raises intracellular calcium, while kisspeptin and leptin provide additional modulatory input. These signals act through scaffold proteins such as PSD95 and CaM and promote phosphorylation of nNOS at Ser1412, allowing conversion of L-arginine to nitric oxide and L-citrulline. Nitric oxide production is linked to changes in the actin cytoskeleton, shown as shifting between G-actin and more polymerized F-actin.\n\nThe key comparison is that pro-oestrus appears to enhance this integrated signaling. Estradiol input from AVPV kisspeptin neurons is shown as stimulatory, and the pathway looks more strongly engaged, with greater receptor coupling, nNOS activation, nitric oxide generation, and actin assembly. Overall, the image suggests that rising estradiol around pro-oestrus strengthens kisspeptin-, glutamate-, and leptin-related signaling to drive structural and functional changes associated with reproductive hormone regulation.",
